 "applies_to": "Seifert Systems enclosure cooling units with Carel, Variable Speed or Seifert Standard controllers, per technical guide RDTG2001 rev 0.2 (09.02.2021).",
 "summary": "Seifert enclosure air conditioners share one Modbus RTU holding-register table across their controller families. Setpoints and temperatures are signed 16-bit values in tenths of a degree, speeds are plain integers, and state, alarm, output and door-contact information are bit fields. Writing the enable-flags register switches the unit from its potentiometer setpoints to network setpoints.",
 "transport": [
  "RTU"
 ],
 "defaults": {
  "unit_id": 1,
  "baud": 19200,
  "parity": "Even",
  "data_bits": 8,
  "stop_bits": 1,
  "tcp_port": null
 },
 "addressing": {
  "convention": "0-based",
  "note": "The table numbers registers from 0 (cooling setpoint) to 27, plus 40 for the address register. A register numbered 0 can only be a protocol address, so the printed numbers are used as wire addresses."
 },
 "default_word_order": null,
 "firmware": null,
 "source": {
  "title": "R&D Technical Guide: Standard Modbus Table",
  "doc_number": "RDTG2001 Rev 0.2",
  "revision_date": "2021-02-09",
  "url": "https://cdn.automationdirect.com/static/manuals/seifertac/modbustable.pdf",
  "pages": "pp. 1-10",
  "unofficial": true
 },
 "notes": [
  "Source host: Seifert does not publish this table on its own site; the copy used is the Seifert document hosted by distributor AutomationDirect.",
  "Every register is 16-bit. S = signed, U = unsigned; a factor of 10 means the raw value is tenths (send 315 for 31.5).",
  "Temperatures are in Celsius unless EN_TEMP_UNIT (bit 11 of register 4) selects Fahrenheit, which changes both the Modbus values and the display.",
  "The document has a per-register availability chart for Unit A (Carel controller), Unit B (Variable Speed controller) and Unit C (Seifert Standard controller); check it, as not every register exists on every unit.",
  "Address 0xFF is a broadcast address the unit always answers; 0x00 and 0xFF cannot be set as the unit address."
 ],
